{"chunk_id": "9c6753f5655d", "text": "Two years after the Pandavas return to Hastinapura, the sage Narada visits King Yudhishthira. When asked about the fate of Dhritarashtra, Narada recounts what happened after the blind king renounced the world. Dhritarashtra, accompanied by Gandhari, Kunti, Sanjaya, and priests, retired to the forests near the Ganga and practiced severe austerities. Emaciated by penance and wandering without attachment, they lived as ascetics. One day a fierce forest fire surrounded them. Too weak to escape, Dhritarashtra calmly ordered Sanjaya to flee while he, Gandhari, and Kunti accepted death as a fitting end for renunciants. Sitting in meditation facing the east, they allowed the flames to consume them. When Yudhishthira hears this, the Pandavas and the royal household erupt in grief, mourning both the old king and Kunti\u2019s tragic end.\nNarada tells Yudhishthira that Dhritarashtra, Gandhari, and Kunti died in a forest fire while practicing severe asceticism; the Pandavas and citizens mourn deeply.\nThemes: ascetic renunciation, death of elders, fate and acceptance, royal grief, forest conflagration", "vector": [0.008291137404739857, -0.01265706866979599, -0.0008345367386937141, 0.00968217570334673, -0.005515947937965393, 0.023069195449352264, -0.011995981447398663, -0.013269951567053795, -0.01361426804214716, -0.010239968076348305, 0.025451863184571266, 0.011954663321375847, 0.015549326315522194, 0.011286689899861813, -0.0044416808523237705, 0.01336636021733284, 0.04547730088233948, -0.005791401024907827, 0.043741948902606964, -0.021003296598792076, 0.0017715075518935919, -0.008215388283133507, -0.007168666459619999, 0.009268996305763721, -0.010494762100279331, 0.01490201149135828, 0.02220151759684086, -0.011148963123559952, 0.0006894934922456741, -0.019502077251672745, -0.004779110662639141, 0.0005255127907730639, 0.012491797097027302, -0.02642972208559513, -0.02662253938615322, 0.007471664808690548, -0.01680263690650463, -0.02386800944